#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# map_at

#% text
# `map_at(f, x, pos)` gives `x` with the part `p` at the position `pos` replaced by `f(p)`.

#% text
# `map_at(f, x, [pos1, pos2, ...])` applies `f` at several positions.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A position is an index or a list of indices, negative from the end; the result is evaluated.

#% item
# `map` applies `f` to every element; `map_at` only at the positions given.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
map_at(f, [a, b, c], 2)
#% output
#= [a, f(b), c]

#% cell
map_at(f, [[a, b], [c, d]], [[1, 2], [2, 1]])
#% output
#= [[a, f(b)], [f(c), d]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A function that evaluates:

#% cell
map_at(x => x^2, [1, 2, 3, 4], [[1], [-1]])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 16]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Applying at every index is `map`:

#% cell
map_at(f, [a, b], [[1], [2]]) == map(f, [a, b])
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `map`, `replace_part`, `extract`, `map_indexed`
